<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 3 – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Interesting Things to See</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Present personal opinions on a variety of topics. Interactive: Engage in problem-solving discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express personal opinions about places to visit by interpreting suggestions and explaining implied meanings. (e.g., "I think when someone says, 'It's popular with tourists,' they really mean it might be overcrowded or too commercial.").<br>Can solve problems related to directions and locations in the city (e.g., "To get to the museum, go straight down this street, take the second left, and it will be on your right.").<br>Can participate in a discussion about planning activities for a friend's visit by asking and answering questions to build a detailed itinerary (e.g., "What time does the museum open?" "After the museum, we can visit the seafood market. What do you think?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of famous Panamanian landmarks and attractions (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Canal, Biomuseo, Taboga Beach, Amador Causeway). Students work in pairs to discuss which places they would recommend to a friend visiting Panama and why. They practice asking for and giving recommendations using phrases like "What places should my friend visit?" and "I would recommend visiting [place] because it's [adjective like 'breathtaking', 'unique', 'interesting']." Students also discuss if they have visited these places using past simple/present perfect (e.g., "I have visited Casco Viejo many times," or "I visited the Panama Canal last year.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing opinions and giving recommendations, explicitly using target vocabulary (e.g., "I think Casco Viejo is a must-see because its history is fascinating," "The Biomuseo showcases our natural heritage.") and target grammar structures. The teacher also models the pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /st/ in "stay," /ks/ in "explain," /tʃ/ in "chance," /ʃ/ in "showcase," and reduced syllables in "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/)."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'The market showcases local crafts,' does 'showcases' mean it hides them or displays them?" (Expected answer: Displays them.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When someone recommends a 'must-see' attraction, are they saying it's optional or important to visit?" (Expected answer: Important to visit.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (or transcript) between two friends, Carla and Roberto, planning an itinerary for their friend, Sara, who is visiting Panama. The dialogue includes asking for recommendations, giving directions, describing attractions, and discussing options.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Dialogue example excerpt:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carla: "So, Sara is arriving next week. What do you think we should plan for her visit?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Roberto: "Well, she said she really wants to see some history. I think Casco Viejo is a must-see. It was once a neglected area, but after its restoration, it has become a popular destination for tourists."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Carla: "Good idea! And what about after that? I was thinking we are going to try that new seafood restaurant by the waterfront."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Roberto: "Perfect! To get to the restaurant from Casco Viejo, we can go straight down Avenue A, then turn left at 5th Street, and it will be across from the old post office. They said that the best seafood is found in the seafood market nearby."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in group planning for events or trips, offering directions and suggestions clearly, ensuring everyone understands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen/read and answer:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> What are Carla and Roberto trying to do? (Expected answer: Plan an itinerary for their friend Sara's visit.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Sara wants to see modern art. (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. They are going to visit Casco Viejo. (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The new restaurant is located next to the museum. (False – across from the old post office)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice expressing personal opinions, giving directions, and planning activities using target grammar (Past simple vs. Present perfect, Future with "will" and "going to," Reported speech), vocabulary (visit, area, location, attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, restaurant, beach, town, history, blocks, cathedral, accommodations, landmark, guide, itinerary, precaution, safety, transportation; see, visit, plan, stay, explore, showcase, witness, connect, miss, recommend, give, explain, guide, describe, enjoy, navigate, direct, advise, arrange; breathtaking, tranquil, interesting, unique, wonderful, must-see, local, national, famous, close, available, safe; go straight, turn right, turn left, across from, next to, between, behind, opposite, in front of), and pronunciation of contrastive sounds and reduced syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Sentence Completion and Oral Production (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks related to planning a visit or giving directions. Students complete the sentences with appropriate vocabulary and grammar, then read them aloud.
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: circle;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To get to the museum, you need to ______ down this street for two ______ and then ______ right." (go straight, blocks, turn)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I ______ the Panama Canal last year, but my friend ______ never ______ it." (visited, has, seen)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They ______ that the fish market is a ______." (said, must-see)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Guided Dialogue Practice (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence starters or cues, and students must complete them verbally, focusing on correct grammar and vocabulary.
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: circle;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Ask your partner for a recommendation for a 'unique' attraction." Student 1: "What ______ do you ______ for a ______ attraction?" Student 2: "I ______ the Biomuseo is ______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Tell your partner what you 'will' do tomorrow." Student 1: "Tomorrow, I ______ a ______ to the beach." Student 2: "That sounds ______! I ______ join you."</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students are encouraged to self-correct and listen for the pronunciation of "interesting" and "restaurant."
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Pair Information Gap & Peer Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a map of a small fictional Panamanian town with different landmarks. Student A has the location of a 'secret attraction' and Student B needs to find it by asking for directions. Student A gives directions using prepositions (e.g., "go straight," "turn left," "across from," "next to"). Students then switch roles. Peer checking involves partners confirming if the directions were clear and if target vocabulary was used.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting Our Country".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Role-Play – "Planning a Friend's Visit to Panama City"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students form groups of three.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Roles: One student is a friend visiting Panama (e.g., "Daniela"), and the other two are local Panamanian friends (e.g., "Sofia" and "Mateo").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The visiting friend expresses interests (e.g., "I want to explore the history," "I'm interested in local food," "I need to find accommodations that are safe").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The local friends must collaboratively plan a detailed one-day itinerary for their friend, covering at least three different attractions (e.g., museum, market, beach, historical area). They need to:
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: circle;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss options and give personal opinions about places to visit (e.g., "I think the seafood market is a wonderful experience," "Casco Viejo has changed so much; it has become very vibrant.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Propose activities using "will" and "going to" (e.g., "We will book a tour of the city," "We are going to try some delicious local dishes").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain implied meanings (e.g., "When people say 'It's a popular attraction,' they sometimes mean it can be crowded, so we should go early.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Give clear directions between locations (e.g., "To get to the museum, go straight down this street, take the second left, and it will be on your right, next to the cathedral").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporate reported speech (e.g., "My cousin told me that the best view is from Cerro Ancón," "The guide said that the canal connects two oceans").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask and answer questions to build the itinerary (e.g., "What time does the museum open?" "After the museum, what do you think we can visit next?").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The goal is to communicate effectively, negotiate plans, and ensure the visitor understands the proposed itinerary and directions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during group role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Planning a Friend's Visit to Panama City" role-play from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Fluency & Coherence:</b> Speaks smoothly, connects ideas logically.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Vocabulary Use:</b> Incorporates a variety of nouns, verbs, adjectives, and prepositions from the recommended list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correctly uses Past simple/Present perfect, Future with "will"/"going to," and Reported speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation & Intelligibility:</b> Clear pronunciation, including attention to contrastive sounds (/st/, /ks/, /tʃ/, /ʃ/) and reduced syllables (e.g., "interesting," "restaurant").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Successfully plans a detailed itinerary, gives clear directions, and expresses opinions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction & Collaboration:</b> Actively participates in group discussion, asks and answers questions, ensures understanding.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I clearly express my opinions and give directions during the activity? How?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging when planning the itinerary and discussing routes with my group, and what did I do to overcome it?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher and student reflection for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new phrases or grammatical structures did I use effectively today to recommend places or give directions?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I improve my fluency and accuracy when discussing travel plans and problem-solving directions in English?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What worked well:</b> The role-play activity significantly engaged students and provided ample opportunity for spontaneous use of the target language. Most students were able to express opinions and give basic directions using relevant vocabulary and the future tense with 'will' and 'going to'. The focus on specific pronunciation points like reduced syllables helped improve intelligibility.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Adjustments/Scaffolds needed for next lesson:</b> Some students struggled with the contrasting usage of Past Simple vs. Present Perfect, and with forming complex sentences using Reported Speech spontaneously. More targeted practice or a quick review session on these specific grammar points might be beneficial for future speaking tasks. I will prepare sentence frames for reported speech for the next lesson’s warm-up.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson provided a solid foundation for vocabulary and idea generation. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing) will build on these ideas, where students will write a travel blog entry or a detailed itinerary description of "Interesting Things to See" for a visitor, incorporating the opinions, directions, and planning discussed today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>